<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary, Queen of Scots</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a>–<a href="/years/1587">1587</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mary Stuart, Mary I of Scotland · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">House of Stuart</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary, Queen of Scots was Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 1542, when she inherited the throne at six days old on the death of James V, until her forced abdication in 1567. Brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, she married the Dauphin Francis in 1558 and was queen consort of France until his death in 1560, then returned to Scotland as the Catholic monarch of a Protestant kingdom. After the murder of Lord Darnley in 1567 and her marriage to the Earl of Bothwell, an uprising forced her to abdicate in favour of her son James VI. She fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> kept her for eighteen and a half years; found guilty of plotting to assassinate Elizabeth, she was beheaded at Fotheringhay Castle in 1587.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots.jpg" alt="Mary, Queen of Scots" width="330" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:François_Clouet_-_Mary,_Queen_of_Scots_(1542-87)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mary was born on 8 December 1542 at Linlithgow Palace to King James V and his French second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-guise">Mary of Guise</a>. Through her grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-tudor">Margaret Tudor</a> she was a great-granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII of England</a>. Scotland was governed by regents, and on 1 July 1543 the Treaty of Greenwich promised that she would marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>'s son Edward. At five she was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, sailing from Dumbarton on 7 August 1548, and she spent thirteen years at the French court, learning lute and virginals, horsemanship and falconry, and French, Italian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Spanish and Greek besides Scots. When Henry II died in 1559 she and Francis became king and queen of France.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> died on 5 December 1560, and Mary arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a> on 19 August 1561. A devout Catholic, she was regarded with suspicion by many subjects, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a> preached against her, but she tolerated the new Protestant ascendancy and kept her Protestant half-brother, the Earl of Moray, as chief adviser. She married Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley, on 29 July 1565, and their son James was born on 19 June 1566. After the murder of her secretary David Rizzio the marriage broke down, and Darnley was found murdered in February 1567. The Earl of Bothwell was acquitted in April and married Mary on 15 May, a match unpopular with Protestants and Catholics alike.</p><p>An uprising followed, and Mary was imprisoned at Lochleven Castle and forced to abdicate on 24 July 1567 in favour of her one-year-old son. She escaped on 2 May 1568, raised about 6,000 men and was defeated by Moray's smaller force at Langside on 13 May. She crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway Firth</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 16 May. At an inquiry Moray presented the casket letters, said to be from Mary to Bothwell; she denied writing them, and historians still dispute their authenticity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Elizabeth confined Mary for eighteen and a half years in castles and manor houses in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In August 1586, implicated in the Babington Plot, she was arrested, and in October a court of 36 noblemen tried her for treason at Fotheringhay. She denied the charges and protested that she had never been an English subject. She was beheaded there on 8 February 1587, her last words being In manus tuas, Domine, commendo spiritum meum. Her courage at her execution helped establish her popular image as the heroic victim in a tragedy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mary, Queen of Scots” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A younger son of René II founded the house of Guise in France, which led the Catholic League during the Wars of Religion and gave Scotland Mary of Guise, mother of <b>Mary, Queen of Scots</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mary, Queen of Scots</b>, married Francis, Dauphin of France and heir to the French throne, at Notre-Dame de Paris.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Anthony Babington, John Ballard and the other men tried with them were hanged, drawn and quartered for plotting to kill Elizabeth I and put <b>Mary, Queen of Scots</b>, on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-20">20 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
